<p>The library of St John&#8217;s College, Oxford, used to hold a copy of Spenser&#8217;s <em>The Faerie Queene</em> that bore a student&#8217;s penciled comment on the last page:</p>
<p>&#8220;First I thought Troilus And Criseyde was the most boring poem in English. Then I thought Beowulf was. Then I thought Paradise Lost was. Now I know that The Faerie Queen is the dullest thing out. Blast it.&#8221;</p>
<p>It had been written by Philip Larkin, who had studied English there in the 1940s.</p>
